I measured the stream-json path first, and the problem the task expected isn't there. So I didn't add the abort policy to stream-json; instead, two tests now lock in its current, correct behaviour. Committed as ef018be4; #989 is closed with a comment naming both commits, and gh issue view 989 shows CLOSED. Formatting, clippy and the full test suite all pass.

What the measurement showed:
• **Partial answer, then a mid-stream rate-limit error, then PONG:** exit 0, 2 requests. `PARTIAL_` does go out on the stream. But the failed attempt is then closed with an error record saying it "will be retried", followed by a retry event. All three final records (messageEnd, turnEnd, agentEnd) contain only PONG\n.
• **429 before any text, then PONG:** an empty error record, a retry event, then PONG, exit 0.
• The full raw output is in the commit message.

Why I left stream-json alone: on a plain-text pipe, the reader can't tell two attempts apart. That's why the text path (Task 1, eaeab54f) now exits non-zero instead. Stream-json marks the failed attempt in the stream itself, so a consumer can discard it. Applying the same abort there would turn every recoverable stream-json run into a failure and fix nothing. The task's own instructions allowed this outcome if the first measurement disproved the hypothesis.

The new tests (in tests/print_stdout_contract.rs, checking exact fields):
• **Mid-stream case:** exit 0 and 2 requests. `PARTIAL_` really appears in the stream, so the test can't pass vacuously. It is followed directly by an error `messageEnd` and then the retry event, and the final answer is exactly PONG\n.
• **429 case:** exit 0, a retry event is present, and the final answer is exactly PONG\n.

Other problems the raw output showed, not fixed:
• yoagent's retry message prints internal Rust values instead of a number (retry after Some(0)ms, Nonems). That's upstream in yoagent and cosmetic.
• yoyo's internal copy of the answer in stream-json mode still joins both attempts into PARTIAL_PONG\n. Nothing reads it today (all three callers only check whether an API error occurred), but a future caller that uses the text would get both attempts.

I ran out of token budget once during this task, so I kept the finish narrow and didn't fix that internal text copy. Both leftovers are named in the commit message and the closing comment on #989.
